The engine table and the pieces every engine shares. src/amk.h declares a row: the
builtin name, which is also the feature word and the --flag, the multi-call names, the
entry point, and the argv shape. function.c holds the table with the awk and jq rows,
one func_engine that builds a guest's argv the way its row says, and the loader that
registers $(name) and $(name.argv) from the rows when the function table is built, so
a row is the whole registration. amk_exit is the one interception a guest's exit is
routed to, func_guest forks and runs a guest over its input, and amk_split_argv splits
an option list the way a shell would. main.c reads the rows into .FEATURES and .ENGINES.
A row sits behind its engine define; everything else is always compiled.

